    "https://careers.centre.edu/channels/employer/": {
      "title": "Employers",
      "description": "Employers – Center for Career & Professional Development | Centre College Skip to content Skip to AI Search Skip to main nav Search Keywords Log in Sign up Guests Access Handshake Make an appointment Who We Serve Alumni & Parents Employers Faculty & Staff Students Career Exploration Communities What is a CEC? Arts, Media & Entertainment Business & Finance Health & Medicine Law, Government & Policy Science, Engineering & Tech Social Impact Build Career Skills Apply to Grad School Create a Resume / Cover Letter Explore Your Interests Find an Internship Find a Job Interviewing Network with Centre Alumni Our Outcomes Meet the Team Meet the Staff Meet the Student Team Employers Centre students graduate with research and internship experience, global perspectives, and rigorous academic work that prepare them for success in a variety of career fields. Internship supervisors report that Centre students display crucial soft skills, including initiative, adaptability, oral and written communication, enthusiasm, professionalism, teamwork, problem-solving, and leadership, making them an asset to any organization. Hire Centre Students In addition to post-graduate job opportunities, we welcome all types of internships for our students. To post an internship or job to Centre students, you can email Laura Hill , or post directly through Handshake. If you would prefer to post your position(s) to Centre students directly through Handshake , the online job and internship posting system used by most colleges, you can do so at no cost here . Full details on using Handshake are below. Once you create an account and post your position(s), you can review and accept applicants, manage interview schedules, and communicate with candidates, all directly and easily through this system. A Guide to Using Handshake Get Involved on Campus We also welcome employers onto campus. Below are a few of the ways employers can connect directly with students. To schedule any of these, simply email Laura Hill . On-campus recruitment Information sessions Tabling Office Hours Class presentations Interviews Resume reviews Reach students through our social media Posting job opportunities, fairs, events, etc. Hosting a live on Instagram Instagram “takeovers” Instagram Facebook YouTube Twitter Sidebar Connect with Us Laura Hill Meet Laura Career Resources Skip filter options to posts Filter Clear Filter: Filters See More Filters Filter Controls Resource Categories: Community Resources Cover Letter/Thank You Resources Employer Resources Faculty & Staff Resources Financial Aid How-tos & Support Guides Job Search Tools Networking Tools Online Tools Professional Associations Resume Resources Student Clubs & Organizations Student Highlighted Resources Close Clear Filters Internship Handbook for Employers Share This: Share Internship Handbook for Employers on Facebook Share Internship Handbook for Employers on LinkedIn Share Internship Handbook for Employers on X Copy Link View Resource How to Post a Job on Handshake Share This: Share How to Post a Job on Handshake on Facebook Share How to Post a Job on Handshake on LinkedIn Share How to Post a Job on Handshake on X Copy Link Centre College provides an online job board, Handshake , for employers to post jobs and internships at no charge to current … Read More Recruiting Interns Share This: Share Recruiting Interns on Facebook Share Recruiting Interns on LinkedIn Share Recruiting Interns on X Copy Link Centre College students have a long tradition of participating in meaningful internships across the country. Last year, over 70 students participated … Read More View All Resources Contact & Location Phone 859.238.5283 Email careers@centre.edu Address Old Carnegie, First floor 600 West Walnut Street Danville, KY 40422 CCPD Hours M Monday 8:30 am - 4:30 pm T Tuesday 8:30 am - 4:30 pm W Wednesday 8:30 am - 4:30 pm TH Thursday 8:30 am - 4:30 pm F Friday 8:30 am - 4:30 pm Meet the Team Jess Metzmeier Meet Jess Joy Asher Meet Joy Kirk Knott Meet Kirk Nate Meissner Meet Nate Lainy Castañeda Meet Lainy Kaitlyn Overstreet Meet Kaitlyn Michael Taylor Meet Michael Laura Hill Meet Laura Genny Ballard Meet Genny Bryana Miracle Meet Bryana Sherri Gowins Meet Sherri Phone 859.238.5283 Email careers@centre.edu Address Old Carnegie, First floor 600 West Walnut Street Danville, KY 40422 Facebook Instagram LinkedIn YouTube Footer Column 1 Blog Events Footer Column 2 Jobs & Internships Resources Meet the Team Privacy Policy | Terms of Service Copyright © 2026 Centre College"
    },
    "https://careers.centre.edu/channels/staff/": {
      "title": "Faculty & Staff",
      "description": "Faculty & Staff – Center for Career & Professional Development | Centre College Skip to content Skip to AI Search Skip to main nav Search Keywords Log in Sign up Guests Access Handshake Make an appointment Who We Serve Alumni & Parents Employers Faculty & Staff Students Career Exploration Communities What is a CEC? Arts, Media & Entertainment Business & Finance Health & Medicine Law, Government & Policy Science, Engineering & Tech Social Impact Build Career Skills Apply to Grad School Create a Resume / Cover Letter Explore Your Interests Find an Internship Find a Job Interviewing Network with Centre Alumni Our Outcomes Meet the Team Meet the Staff Meet the Student Team News & Advice Career Resources Student Resources Jobs & Internships Career Videos Our Outcomes Faculty & Staff Help a Student Find their Community Want to know more about the Career Exploration Communities (CECs) and how they can benefit students? Learn all about the CECs here. What is a CEC? Is your student having trouble deciding which community is right for them? They can take our CEC quiz to get a percentage match with each CEC! Take the CEC Quiz Connecting with the CCPD What Happens When I Send a Student to the CCPD? Watch this short video to see what happens, start to finish, when students engage with our office! How to Connect with the CCPD Do you have questions but don’t know who to contact? We’ve created a guide to help you connect with the correct CCPD staff member based on your needs! CCPD Staff Guide Faculty Career Champions Meet the 2025-26 Faculty Career Champions cohort (pictured above, left to right): Andy Brown, Andrew Hitron, Zach Klobnak, Sami Sweis, Kane Anderson, Melissa Burns-Cusato, Shea Carr, Jamie Shenton, and Weiss Mehrabi. About Faculty Career Champions Each year, a new cohort of 8-10 faculty will spend the fall learning about the NACE competencies and how to integrate these into the classroom to help students connect the dots between what they’re learning in their classes (whether gen ed, electives, or major classes) and their career success.  These faculty will also spend time discussing how better to facilitate students’ career readiness during academic advising.  In the spring, each cohort will then integrate what they learned into their courses and their advising; providing feedback at the end of the spring to help strengthen the work done with future cohorts. Get Involved If you’re a faculty member who’d like to learn more about the NACE competencies, integrating career readiness into your classes, or answering students’ career questions during advising, please feel free to reach out to any of the current cohort members or to Amy Frederick , lead faculty career champion. Also check out the Career Resources for Faculty section on Moodle Spaces for information about the competencies, sample syllabi and in-class assignments from your peers, and a list of alumni willing to talk in classes. Career Resources for Faculty The Career Resources for Faculty Moodle Space provides resources including: a list of alumni interested in speaking in classes, a list of alumni willing to do information interviews with students, readings for faculty on career readiness, and ideas and samples of syllabi and activities integrating career readiness into your courses. Career Resources for Faculty Serve as a Faculty Mentor for Academic-Credit Internships Academic-credit internships require the supervision of a Centre faculty member and an on-site supervisor, combining a substantive academic component with a significant career-related component to bridge the liberal arts education and workplace expectations. Academic Requirement & Grades In academic-credit internships, the academic component must be an integral part of the work experience. It is designed by the faculty mentor in consultation with the student and, if possible, the employer. This component might include a paper, a journal, a portfolio, or written notes from a series of interviews. Readings, assigned by the faculty mentor and by the employer sponsor, are generally also required. The academic component must be specified in the internship learning agreement . Acceptable performance on the academic component and on-site work will allow a student to earn a “credit” designation for the appropriate number of academic credits. This credit designation will be based on the completion of all required internship hours, the academic component, and any paperwork required by the Center for Career & Professional Development (timecards, evaluations, etc.), as well as a satisfactory final evaluation completed by the site supervisor. Credit designation does not count for or against the requirements for a major program or the pass/fail option. Students who do not perform at an acceptable level in either area will be assigned a no-credit designation. This will appear on the student’s transcript. Eligibility A student who wishes to enroll in an academic-credit internship must have at least sophomore status. Variable internship credit is awarded according to hours worked. One hour of credit is awarded for every 40 hours worked per term, meaning that students working 120 total hours over the duration of the term will receive three credit hours for that term. During the fall and spring, Centre College typically offers two- and three-credit internships. Two-credit internships require 80 total hours of work (6-7 hours per week), and three-credit internships require 120 total hours of work (10 hours per week). All work must be sanctioned by and done on behalf of the internship supervisor and must be documented on a time card provided by the college. If conducted during CentreTerm, the internship is full-time for three to four weeks for a total of 120 work hours and is not done while taking another course. If the CentreTerm internship lasts for more than three weeks, the internship will need to either begin before the actual beginning of the term or, more likely, last beyond the official end of term. Internships completed during the fall and spring terms may provide a more comprehensive exposure to the sponsoring organization, while an internship completed during CentreTerm may be more intensive and more closely resemble the eight-hour-day work schedule. The internship must start during the first week of the term. At the beginning of the term, students will attend a required group meeting with the other students enrolled in an internship. For CentreTerm, this meeting will take place at the end of the fall term. Student Requirements During the Internship Attend a pre-internship meeting. This meeting will provide and introduction to experiential education and review the requirements for the internship. Midterm Evaluations. Both the student and the supervisor will complete a short evaluation at the mid-point of the internship. Timecards. Each student will be required to complete a timecard and submit it with the supervisor’s signature at midterm and the end of the term. Final Assessments. Both the student and the supervisor will complete a final assessment. Internship Showcase Presentation. At the end of the term, the student will participate in an internship showcase. Faculty, staff, and supervisors will be invited to attend. Students will be expected to talk about the internship experience with the Centre community and will need to provide a visual aid. This is an open house format. Completion of all academic requirements as assigned by the faculty mentor. Consistent attendance with a satisfactory final evaluation. Forms & Deadlines All students must complete an Internship Learning Agreements prior to the start of their internship. Academic-Credit Internship Learning Agreement Deadlines: Fall 2024 Internship – September 1, 2024 CentreTerm 2025 Internship – December 10, 2024 Spring 2025 Internship – December 10, 2024 Summer 2025 Internship – May 1 **Please note that students are encouraged to submit their Internship Learning Agreement before the deadlines stated above to allow for ample time to process and obtain approvals. Internship Funding Proposal Deadlines: CentreTerm 2025 – November 11, 2024 Summer 2025 – April 10, 2025 Sidebar Connect with Us Joy Asher Meet Joy Faculty Career Champions Lead Career Champion, Amy Frederick Send an Email Career Resources Skip filter options to posts Filter Clear Filter: Filters See More Filters Filter Controls Resource Categories: Community Resources Cover Letter/Thank You Resources Employer Resources Faculty & Staff Resources Financial Aid How-tos & Support Guides Job Search Tools Networking Tools Online Tools Professional Associations Resume Resources Student Clubs & Organizations Student Highlighted Resources Close Clear Filters Internship Learning Agreements Share This: Share Internship Learning Agreements on Facebook Share Internship Learning Agreements on LinkedIn Share Internship Learning Agreements on X Copy Link Centre College requires that students complete their internship learning agreements online through Docusign. This system allows students to complete contracts … Read More Career Explorations Quiz Share This: Share Career Explorations Quiz on Facebook Share Career Explorations Quiz on LinkedIn Share Career Explorations Quiz on X Copy Link Confused about which CEC a student should join? Take this quiz to find out! View Resource Internship Funding Share This: Share Internship Funding on Facebook Share Internship Funding on LinkedIn Share Internship Funding on X Copy Link Centre College provides competitive financial support for students completing high-caliber unpaid or low-paid domestic or international internships during the CentreTerm … Read More Available Internship Funds Share This: Share Available Internship Funds on Facebook Share Available Internship Funds on LinkedIn Share Available Internship Funds on X Copy Link Centre College’s internship funding program consists of numerous awards to support student internships. Once th